/* book Worked example 2: Q: Benzene(1)–toluene(2) is close to ideal. Find the bubble temperature and the first-vapour composition of a liquid with x₁ = 0.50 at 101.325 kPa. Antoine constants (ln P in kPa, T in °C): benzene A = 13.7819, B = 2726.81, C = 217.572; toluene A = 13.9320, B = 3056.96, C = 217.625.
   1. Saturation temperatures at 101.325 kPa, from T = B/(A − ln P) − C with ln 101.325 = 4.6183: benzene 2726.81/9.1636 − 217.572 = 80.0 °C; toluene 3056.96/9.3137 − 217.625 = 110.6 °C.
   2. First guess, the mole-fraction average: T = 0.5 × 80.0 + 0.5 × 110.6 = 95.3 °C. Then P₁sat = 158.62 kPa, P₂sat = 64.26 kPa and ΣxiPisat = 79.31 + 32.13 = 111.44 kPa, too high, so T is too high.
   3. Second guess T = 90.0 °C: P₁sat = 136.50, P₂sat = 54.31 kPa, sum = 95.40 kPa, too low.
   4. Interpolate: T = 90.0 + 5.3 × (101.325 − 95.40)/(111.44 − 95.40) = 91.96 °C, which gives a sum of 101.10 kPa. One more step gives T = 92.03 °C with P₁sat = 144.67 kPa, P₂sat = 57.97 kPa and a sum of 101.32 kPa.
   5. First vapour: y₁ = x₁P₁sat/P = 0.50 × 144.67/101.325 = 0.714.
   Answer: The bubble temperature is 92.0 °C (365.2 K), and the first vapour has y₁ = 0.714. */
