/* book exam traps: Applying plain Raoult's law to a pair with a known azeotrope. Ideal Raoult's law cannot produce an azeotrope at all unless the two Pisat are equal, so it will badly misfit ethanol–water. | Evaluating γi at the wrong composition. In the one-parameter Margules model γ₁ uses x₂² and γ₂ uses x₁²; swapping the two squares is the commonest slip in the exam hall. | Reading a dew point as a bubble point. At a dew point the given composition is y; substituting it as x gives a liquid far richer in the heavy component and the wrong pressure. */
